2
00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:07.374
[suspenseful music]

3
00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:15.406
♪♪

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.074
Advertise your product or brand here
contact www.OpenSubtitles.org today

5
00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:47.980
- No. it's good. It's good.

6
00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:48.981
- It's all right.
It's all right.

7
00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:51.609
- Nice out there.
- Yeah.

8
00:00:51.652 --> 00:00:53.396
Are you all right? You
took that hit pretty hard.

9
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.754
- Oh. I think I'll survive.
[laughs]

10
00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.157
[tapping]

11
00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.497
- Damn it. Driscoll. stop that!

12
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:06.227
- It's called control.

13
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.894
I'd hazard you could
learn some of that.

14
00:01:07.935 --> 00:01:09.301
- As could you. Eddie.

15
00:01:09.336 --> 00:01:11.305
You almost injured three
of your teammates today.

16
00:01:11.338 --> 00:01:13.830
- The Shamrocks. the Victorias.
they come at you in waves.

17
00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:15.000
We need to be ready.

18
00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.600
- [sighs]

19
00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:19.741
- Captain.

20
00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.416
- I said stop that.

21
00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:26.896
- He's still smarting
after being made

22
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.040
to look the fool on the ice.

23
00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:29.989
- Watch your mouth. Driscoll.
- Easy. boys.

24
00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.491
We all play for the same side.

25
00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:32.788
- Not all of us.

26
00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:34.795
Driscoll here doesn't
play for the team.

27
00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.319
He plays for himself.

28
00:01:36.363 --> 00:01:37.563
- And you're lucky to have me.

29
00:01:37.598 --> 00:01:38.908
- You care nothing
for the science

30
00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:40.052
and strategies of this game.

31
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.193
- Science my arse.

32
00:01:41.268 --> 00:01:42.463
I skate like the devil

33
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.630
and get the puck
past the goalkeeper.

34
00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:46.537
which I haven't
seen you doing lately.

35
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.041
- Take it easy. Take it easy.

36
00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:54.046
Captain. you all right?

37
00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.275
- What is wrong with you?

38
00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.113
- Simpson. man.

39
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.550
Come on. wake up.

40
00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:04.014
- [groaning]

41
00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.793
- Sorry. Simpson.
I didn't think.

42
00:02:13.834 --> 00:02:16.736
- That's your problem. Eddie.

43
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.139
- Looks like we
could all use a drink.

44
00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:24.235
The Mongoose and Ferret?

45
00:02:24.311 --> 00:02:27.247
- I'll be there presently.

46
00:02:27.314 --> 00:02:28.314
Ow.

47
00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:44.089
- Crabapple.

48
00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:47.891
- Eddie Driscoll.
Good to see you.

49
00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:50.995
- Well. aren't you a
sight for sore eyes.

50
00:02:51.038 --> 00:02:52.836
Sorry I kept you
waiting. Georgie boy.

51
00:02:52.873 --> 00:02:54.398
I had a little errand to run.

52
00:02:54.441 --> 00:02:55.409
- Quite all right.

53
00:02:55.442 --> 00:02:57.343
- It's on the house. sir.

54
00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.175
- And my friend here?

55
00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:00.407
- Of course.

56
00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:02.507
- Not bad for a working
man. eh. George?

57
00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:03.881
- You know. I
read in <i>The Gazette</i>

58
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:06.182
that somebody by your name
had joined the Wellingtons.

59
00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.949
Thank you. I was
sure it would be you.

60
00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.217
- Been here a month.

61
00:03:10.257 --> 00:03:12.692
Look at you now.
a big city copper.

62
00:03:12.726 --> 00:03:14.456
- And a mystery novelist.

63
00:03:14.528 --> 00:03:15.791
I published a novel.

64
00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.421
I brought you an
autographed copy.

65
00:03:17.464 --> 00:03:19.695
Now. I signed it
to you and Lydia.

66
00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:21.224
I wasn't sure if...

67
00:03:21.268 --> 00:03:23.362
- Married last year.

68
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.372
- Married?

69
00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:26.532
That's fantastic.

70
00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:28.439
I'm very happy for you both.

71
00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:30.967
- Well. what can I say?

72
00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:33.707
I guess the best man won.

73
00:03:33.747 --> 00:03:36.717
[suspenseful music]

74
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:41.711
♪♪

75
00:03:41.755 --> 00:03:43.280
- His name is Archibald Simpson.

76
00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.588
He was found at 6:15
by one of his teammates.

77
00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.253
Mr. Jerome Bradley.

78
00:03:47.294 --> 00:03:49.559
- I've yet to take his
internal temperature.

79
00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:51.326
so I can't give you a
precise time of death.

80
00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:54.332
- And the cause?

81
00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:55.402
- Uncertain.

82
00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:57.802
but he appears to
have suffered an injury

83
00:03:57.838 --> 00:03:59.898
to the back of the head.

84
00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.884
- According to Mr. Bradley.
sir. the victim was involved

85
00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.400
in a dustup after the
afternoon practice.

86
00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:05.935
- Where is this Mr. Bradley?

87
00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.140
- He's just outside.

88
00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.671
- Bring him in. Henry.

89
00:04:08.749 --> 00:04:11.014
- Jerome Bradley?

90
00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:12.814
- You know him?

91
00:04:12.853 --> 00:04:14.116
- When I was younger.

92
00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:20.859
- Emily.

93
00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:22.418
- Hello. Jerome.

94
00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:25.626
- Mr. Bradley. I understand
that Mr. Simpson

95
00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:27.634
was involved in
a scuffle earlier.

96
00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:30.535
- Yes. he took a punch
and bashed his head open

97
00:04:30.604 --> 00:04:31.765
on that post there.

98
00:04:31.805 --> 00:04:34.468
There was quite a lot of blood.

99
00:04:34.508 --> 00:04:36.101
He came to. though.

100
00:04:36.143 --> 00:04:37.702
- What time was this?

101
00:04:37.778 --> 00:04:40.145
- 5:00 or thereabouts.

102
00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.457
You think that's
what killed him?

103
00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.541
- If he suffered
subdural bleeding.

104
00:04:43.617 --> 00:04:45.518
death could have been
delayed an hour or more.

105
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.382
- Who delivered the punch?

106
00:04:48.455 --> 00:04:50.686
- Eddie Driscoll is his name.

107
00:04:50.724 --> 00:04:53.193
- Where might I
find this Mr. Driscoll?

108
00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.388
- At the Mongoose and Ferret.

109
00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:57.722
- Eddie Driscoll?

110
00:04:57.798 --> 00:04:59.892
Pleasure to meet you.

111
00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.332
Buy you a drink?

112
00:05:01.368 --> 00:05:04.463
- I never say no to another.

113
00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:06.182
- Really gave it to
those boys from Ottawa.

114
00:05:06.206 --> 00:05:07.572
- That I did.

115
00:05:07.641 --> 00:05:08.641
Now. if you'll excuse me.

116
00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:10.337
I'm talking to my
good friend here.

117
00:05:12.579 --> 00:05:14.480
Well. been a happy
man since I discovered

118
00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:16.312
the true love of my life.

119
00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:18.215
Or should I say.
since it found me?

120
00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:20.015
- It?

121
00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:22.921
- Hockey of course.

122
00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:24.252
- Detective Murdoch.

123
00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.656
Oh. please. make
the acquaintance

124
00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:27.558
of my good friend
Eddie Driscoll.

125
00:05:27.594 --> 00:05:29.722
- It's a pleasure to
meet you. Mr. Driscoll.

126
00:05:29.763 --> 00:05:31.197
I wonder if you
could accompany me

127
00:05:31.231 --> 00:05:33.666
to Station House number 4.

128
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.601
- The station? Why?

129
00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:38.002
- I'm placing you under
arrest for manslaughter.

130
00:05:47.581 --> 00:05:49.948
- He came at me first. George.
I simply clocked him back.

131
00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:52.110
He was alive when we left him.

132
00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:54.279
- It will all get sorted out.

133
00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.898
Do you want me to
contact Lydia for you?

134
00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.584
- No.

135
00:05:57.624 --> 00:05:59.115
I won't have her see me here.

136
00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.854
- Maybe a solicitor. then?

137
00:06:02.896 --> 00:06:04.728
- You think I'll be needing one?

138
00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:10.636
- The players all agreed to
meet at a tavern after the practice.

139
00:06:10.704 --> 00:06:12.605
but Simpson never arrived.

140
00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:14.130
- Who saw him last?

141
00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:15.266
- A Samuel Farrell.

142
00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:16.348
but he claims that Simpson

143
00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:17.776
was very much
alive and conversive

144
00:06:17.811 --> 00:06:19.575
when he last saw him.

145
00:06:19.613 --> 00:06:22.310
- Murdoch. if this is
just a matter of fisticuffs.

146
00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:24.044
don't you think that a
manslaughter charge

147
00:06:24.084 --> 00:06:25.245
is a little harsh?

148
00:06:25.285 --> 00:06:27.754
- Sir. a man is dead
as a direct consequence

149
00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:29.551
of a violent assault.

150
00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:33.390
By definition. that
is manslaughter.

151
00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.327
- If you ask me. they
should lock him up.

152
00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.328
- Why?

153
00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:38.558
- Driscoll is nothing
more than a thug.

154
00:06:38.598 --> 00:06:40.089
a lowlife ruffian.

155
00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:42.500
and he hardly belongs
in a sport for gentlemen.

156
00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:44.003
- This scuffle in
the locker room.

157
00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.301
who started it?

158
00:06:46.339 --> 00:06:47.637
- Well. Simpson. I suppose.

159
00:06:47.674 --> 00:06:48.818
but Eddie Driscoll
certainly brought it

160
00:06:48.842 --> 00:06:50.640
to a conclusion. didn't he?

161
00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.675
- Who's in charge here?

162
00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.345
- That would be me.
And who might you be?

163
00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.785
- Langston Wallace.

164
00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.585
I'm the owner of
the Wellingtons.

165
00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:11.125
Manslaughter? That's ridiculous.

166
00:07:11.164 --> 00:07:13.190
Eddie's my best player.

167
00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:14.266
- He's your best player.

168
00:07:14.301 --> 00:07:16.998
therefore. he couldn't possibly
have committed manslaughter?

169
00:07:17.037 --> 00:07:19.370
- All he did was punch
him in the jaw once.

170
00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.183
- Were you even in the
room when it occurred?

171
00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:23.335
- I heard enough to
know it was an accident.

172
00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:25.345
and Simpson was up.
walking. talking right after

173
00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:26.539
like nothing had happened.

174
00:07:26.613 --> 00:07:28.639
I spoke to him myself.

175
00:07:28.682 --> 00:07:31.151
- All points for a
jury to deliberate.

176
00:07:31.184 --> 00:07:32.777
- You don't understand.

177
00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:34.685
You see. the Toronto Wellies

178
00:07:34.721 --> 00:07:36.849
are about the challenge
the Montreal Shamrocks

179
00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.121
for Lord Stanley's
coveted trophy.

180
00:07:39.159 --> 00:07:41.321
There's to be an
announcement at a luncheon.

181
00:07:41.361 --> 00:07:43.159
- I don't see the relevance.

182
00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:44.687
- The relevance is this.

183
00:07:44.731 --> 00:07:46.029
With Eddie. we win the Cup.

184
00:07:46.066 --> 00:07:47.728
Without him. we lose.

185
00:07:48.735 --> 00:07:50.397
- I appreciate your position.

186
00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:51.904
- I don't think you do.

187
00:07:51.972 --> 00:07:55.238
You see. no team from
Toronto has ever won the Cup.

188
00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:57.504
a situation that
your chief constable

189
00:07:57.544 --> 00:07:59.672
has an active
interest in rectifying.

190
00:07:59.713 --> 00:08:01.147
- A fan. is he?

191
00:08:01.181 --> 00:08:03.013
- Our biggest.

192
00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:06.486
So if you appreciate
your position. you...

193
00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:07.578
- Listen to me.

194
00:08:07.654 --> 00:08:08.965
I don't give a toss
which team flag

195
00:08:08.989 --> 00:08:11.151
Chief Constable
Giles likes to fly.

196
00:08:11.191 --> 00:08:12.159
- Sir.

197
00:08:12.192 --> 00:08:13.402
- And I don't take
kindly to threats.

198
00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.895
- Inspector Brackenreid.

199
00:08:15.929 --> 00:08:17.329
Langston. my apologies.

200
00:08:17.364 --> 00:08:19.765
- Your apologies are
unnecessary. Percival.

201
00:08:19.833 --> 00:08:21.927
- So what exactly
is going on here?

202
00:08:22.002 --> 00:08:25.029
- I've arrested Eddie Driscoll
on the charge of manslaughter.

203
00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:26.403
- On what grounds?

204
00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:28.908
- He assaulted Mr. Simpson.

205
00:08:28.942 --> 00:08:31.844
and later Mr. Simpson
died of his injuries.

206
00:08:31.878 --> 00:08:34.109
- Langston. let me
talk with my men here.

207
00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:35.773
I'll keep you appraised.

208
00:08:35.849 --> 00:08:37.010
- Thank you.

209
00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.279
I'll wait for you.

210
00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:42.450
- Sir. under the circumstances.

211
00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:45.082
we're required by law to
charge him with manslaughter.

212
00:08:45.125 --> 00:08:46.957
- By law. is it?

213
00:08:47.027 --> 00:08:50.794
Strange how a confessed murderer
can slip freely from your cells.

214
00:08:50.864 --> 00:08:53.060
but a young man who
lands an unlucky punch.

215
00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:56.797
well. the letter of the law
simply must be applied.

216
00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:58.395
- Are you suggesting
it shouldn't?

217
00:08:58.438 --> 00:09:00.100
- Do you know for
a fact that Simpson

218
00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:01.733
died from that punch?

219
00:09:01.775 --> 00:09:04.074
- Dr. Grace has yet to
complete her postmortem but...

220
00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.080
- I have to go to an important
luncheon. as does Mr. Wallace.

221
00:09:07.113 --> 00:09:09.480
When I return. you will
present me with proof

222
00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:11.450
that Eddie Driscoll
has committed a crime.

223
00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:14.648
or you will release him.

224
00:09:23.229 --> 00:09:25.164
- Dr. Grace.

225
00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:26.756
Have you confirmed
the cause of death?

226
00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.268
- I have indeed.

227
00:09:28.301 --> 00:09:30.770
Take a look at this.

228
00:09:30.804 --> 00:09:34.002
This is the injury he received
when his head hit the post.

229
00:09:34.074 --> 00:09:37.476
This is the blow
that killed him.

230
00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.809
- Are you saying he
received another blow

231
00:09:39.846 --> 00:09:41.906
after the incident
in the locker room?

232
00:09:41.948 --> 00:09:43.507
- Based on the bruising.

233
00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:45.745
I'd estimate the fatal
blow was delivered

234
00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:47.515
about half an hour later.

235
00:09:47.587 --> 00:09:51.354
which conforms to my best
estimate for a time of death.

236
00:09:51.424 --> 00:09:52.858
- And what time would that be?

237
00:09:52.926 --> 00:09:54.861
- Approximately 6:00.

238
00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:58.622
- Which means this
was no accidental death

239
00:09:58.665 --> 00:10:00.861
caused by a locker room scuffle.

240
00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.430
- Gentlemen. our
final order of business

241
00:10:06.473 --> 00:10:07.583
will be to strike a committee

242
00:10:07.607 --> 00:10:09.701
to see if we can
find a unity of rules

243
00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:12.211
for each hockey organization.

244
00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:14.543
I pledge that next year

245
00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:16.674
all hockey leagues
will follow the example

246
00:10:16.716 --> 00:10:19.652
of our Ontario
Hockey Association

247
00:10:19.686 --> 00:10:22.713
and adopt the use
of the hockey net.

248
00:10:22.789 --> 00:10:25.782
[applause]

249
00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:28.537
- It's not the rules we
should worry about.

250
00:10:28.561 --> 00:10:30.154
It's the damn professionals.

251
00:10:30.196 --> 00:10:31.664
- There won't be professionals

252
00:10:31.698 --> 00:10:34.998
as long as John Ross
Robertson is in charge.

253
00:10:35.034 --> 00:10:37.833
But we may lose some of our
best players to the Americans.

254
00:10:37.871 --> 00:10:39.396
- Oh. it's our game.

255
00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:41.304
The Yanks will never embrace it.

256
00:10:41.341 --> 00:10:44.641
Besides. if you pay a
man to play a game.

257
00:10:44.677 --> 00:10:46.703
who will work the factories?

258
00:10:46.746 --> 00:10:47.805
- Hmm.

259
00:10:47.847 --> 00:10:49.145
[glass clinking]

260
00:10:49.182 --> 00:10:52.311
I have an announcement
to make. gentlemen. if I may.

261
00:10:52.352 --> 00:10:53.820
As you all know.

262
00:10:53.853 --> 00:10:56.084
the other day in
a tragic accident.

263
00:10:56.156 --> 00:10:58.421
we lost Archie Simpson.

264
00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:01.552
the revered captain
of our great team.

265
00:11:01.594 --> 00:11:03.893
The Iron Dukes of Toronto.
the Toronto Wellingtons.

266
00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:08.698
well. we still have the best
hockey team in all the land.

267
00:11:08.735 --> 00:11:10.727
[cheers. murmuring]

268
00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:14.366
Yes. yes. And we
are going to prove it.

269
00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:18.276
Our newly appointed
captain. Mr. Jerome Bradley.

270
00:11:18.344 --> 00:11:22.509
will lead the Toronto
Wellingtons' charge

271
00:11:22.549 --> 00:11:26.077
as we challenge the Montreal
Shamrocks for the Cup.

272
00:11:26.119 --> 00:11:27.519
[applause]

273
00:11:27.554 --> 00:11:30.854
- You mean Lord
Stanley's punch bowl.

274
00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.882
[laughter]

275
00:11:32.926 --> 00:11:35.760
The closest you will ever
get to the Cup. Wallace.

276
00:11:35.795 --> 00:11:39.391
is when we hoist it
over your head in victory.

277
00:11:39.432 --> 00:11:41.594
- We'll see about
that. McLaughlin.

278
00:11:41.634 --> 00:11:44.604
Archie Simpson will
be watching over us

279
00:11:44.637 --> 00:11:47.971
as we show the Shamrocks
who plays the best hockey.

280
00:11:48.041 --> 00:11:50.738
[crowd murmuring]

281
00:11:50.777 --> 00:11:52.473
Well. what do you
think of our chances?

282
00:11:52.545 --> 00:11:53.979
- Greatly improved.

283
00:11:54.047 --> 00:11:56.915
This Driscoll fellow is
a crackerjack player.

284
00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.545
- Mr. Wallace.
- Yes. he is.

285
00:11:59.586 --> 00:12:00.781
- Excuse me.

286
00:12:05.959 --> 00:12:07.825
What's the meaning
of this intrusion?

287
00:12:07.894 --> 00:12:09.988
- Chief Constable. I
regret to inform you

288
00:12:10.063 --> 00:12:13.261
that Mr. Simpson's
death was no accident.

289
00:12:13.299 --> 00:12:14.733
It was murder.

290
00:12:16.336 --> 00:12:20.432
Mr. Wallace. I'm afraid your
run for the Cup will have to wait.

291
00:12:25.178 --> 00:12:27.841
- It's about time. I'd say.

292
00:12:27.914 --> 00:12:30.008
- Mr. Driscoll.
- Yes?

293
00:12:30.083 --> 00:12:31.449
- Would you sign this for me?

294
00:12:38.091 --> 00:12:40.117
- You gonna give it
to the Shamrocks?

295
00:12:40.159 --> 00:12:42.856
- Do my best.
- Move along now. son.

296
00:12:42.929 --> 00:12:44.795
- You see that. George?

297
00:12:44.831 --> 00:12:47.198
Your detective
Murdoch arrested a hero.

298
00:12:47.267 --> 00:12:48.667
- I'm sorry. Eddie.

299
00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:50.431
Detective Murdoch
did what was required.

300
00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.133
- Be that as it may. I
missed a full practice.

301
00:12:53.172 --> 00:12:54.299
- Eddie. a man is dead.

302
00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:55.774
- It's all very sad.

303
00:12:55.808 --> 00:12:58.004
but it wasn't me that
killed him. was it?

304
00:12:58.044 --> 00:12:59.706
Besides. he was
hardly good enough

305
00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:01.372
to be worth all this fuss.

306
00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.384
- Dinner is the
least you owe me.

307
00:13:05.451 --> 00:13:08.148
You did. after all. leave me
standing bereft at the altar.

308
00:13:08.187 --> 00:13:10.281
- That's not exactly
true. Jerome.

309
00:13:10.323 --> 00:13:12.554
I informed you well
before the wedding day.

310
00:13:12.625 --> 00:13:14.821
- The invitations were
already printed and addressed.

311
00:13:14.861 --> 00:13:16.124
- But not yet mailed out.

312
00:13:16.162 --> 00:13:19.189
- Nonetheless. all expected
to be dancing with us

313
00:13:19.232 --> 00:13:22.168
at our wedding.

314
00:13:22.201 --> 00:13:25.069
- I never meant to
hurt or humiliate you.

315
00:13:25.138 --> 00:13:26.181
But there is nothing
to be regained

316
00:13:26.205 --> 00:13:28.640
by attempting to rekindle that
which was never meant to be.

317
00:13:28.675 --> 00:13:31.338
- I agree. Emily. Let's
let bygones be bygones.

318
00:13:31.377 --> 00:13:35.371
Please indulge me with your
company over dinner tonight.

319
00:13:35.415 --> 00:13:37.748
Just as old friends.

320
00:13:37.817 --> 00:13:39.843
That's what we are now. right?

321
00:13:42.488 --> 00:13:43.751
- All right.

322
00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.092
- Detective.

323
00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:54.258
- Dr. Grace.

324
00:13:54.334 --> 00:13:57.566
why are you entertaining
a potential suspect here?

325
00:13:57.603 --> 00:13:59.572
- I didn't invite
him. Detective.

326
00:13:59.605 --> 00:14:01.016
and I certainly wasn't
entertaining him.

327
00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:03.737
- Under no circumstances
is a suspect to be allowed

328
00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:05.677
anywhere near evidence.

329
00:14:05.712 --> 00:14:07.340
- It won't happen again.

330
00:14:10.183 --> 00:14:11.412
- You have something for me?

331
00:14:11.451 --> 00:14:14.944
- I believe it may be
able to help identify

332
00:14:15.021 --> 00:14:16.455
the murder weapon.

333
00:14:16.522 --> 00:14:20.789
I found these in
Mr. Simpson's hair.

334
00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.261
- Wood slivers.
- Exactly.

335
00:14:23.296 --> 00:14:24.840
You are looking for
a long. thin implement

336
00:14:24.864 --> 00:14:26.355
made of wood.

337
00:14:26.399 --> 00:14:29.426
- I believe I know where I
might be able to find one.

338
00:14:29.469 --> 00:14:30.596
Thank you. Doctor.

339
00:14:33.139 --> 00:14:34.265
- A hockey stick.

340
00:14:34.307 --> 00:14:36.776
Sir. it pains me that
such a joyful apparatus

341
00:14:36.809 --> 00:14:38.402
could be used in
such a terrible way.

342
00:14:38.444 --> 00:14:40.255
- Not as much as it pains
me that they cleaned up

343
00:14:40.279 --> 00:14:41.303
this locker room.

344
00:14:41.381 --> 00:14:43.907
Have a look at this. George.

345
00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:48.786
Simpson was found here
with his back to his locker.

346
00:14:48.821 --> 00:14:50.466
- It would be hard to strike
him in the back of the head

347
00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:51.719
if he was there. sir.

348
00:14:51.758 --> 00:14:53.556
- Exactly.

349
00:14:53.593 --> 00:14:56.427
I believe his body
was moved here.

350
00:14:56.462 --> 00:14:58.658
- Why not just leave
him on the floor?

351
00:14:58.731 --> 00:15:01.394
- Clearly. the killer wanted
us to think that Simpson died

352
00:15:01.434 --> 00:15:04.893
of his earlier injury.

353
00:15:04.937 --> 00:15:07.463
- [chuckling] Sir. look at this.

354
00:15:07.507 --> 00:15:09.908
- What?
- Oh. these skate marks.

355
00:15:09.942 --> 00:15:12.173
It appears that one of
the best skaters in this city

356
00:15:12.245 --> 00:15:15.272
walks like a duck
when he's off the ice.

357
00:15:15.314 --> 00:15:17.249
- Have you finished
labeling the sticks. George?

358
00:15:17.283 --> 00:15:18.342
- Yes. sir.

359
00:15:18.418 --> 00:15:19.728
They're all marked
according to their owner.

360
00:15:19.752 --> 00:15:21.015
- Shall we?

361
00:15:23.022 --> 00:15:24.684
- What have you got. Murdoch?

362
00:15:24.757 --> 00:15:27.625
- Sir. I believe one
of these hockey sticks

363
00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.185
to be the murder weapon.

364
00:15:29.262 --> 00:15:31.458
The task before us is
to figure out which one.

365
00:15:31.497 --> 00:15:32.988
- How are you going to do that?

366
00:15:33.032 --> 00:15:35.501
- Through chemi-phosphoro
luminescence. sir.

367
00:15:35.535 --> 00:15:38.471
You see. sir. this compound
will emit a blue glow

368
00:15:38.504 --> 00:15:40.837
when it's oxidized.

369
00:15:40.873 --> 00:15:43.035
In this case. the iron
and the hemoglobin

370
00:15:43.109 --> 00:15:45.135
will act as the catalyst.

371
00:15:45.178 --> 00:15:48.148
The effect should only
last about half a minute.

372
00:15:48.181 --> 00:15:49.525
but it will be long
enough to tell us

373
00:15:49.549 --> 00:15:52.280
which one of these sticks
has been exposed to blood.

374
00:15:52.318 --> 00:15:55.015
Now. sir. if you could
turn out the light.

375
00:15:58.658 --> 00:16:00.058
[lamp buzzing]

376
00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.219
- Bloody hell.

377
00:16:02.295 --> 00:16:04.730
It's a tough sport.
I'll give it that.

378
00:16:04.797 --> 00:16:07.824
- Any one of these sticks
could be the murder weapon.

379
00:16:09.535 --> 00:16:10.815
- What are you
doing in the dark?

380
00:16:15.374 --> 00:16:16.740
- What have you. George?

381
00:16:16.809 --> 00:16:19.244
- Sir. McTavish.
Danton. and McKenna

382
00:16:19.312 --> 00:16:20.871
were all at the
Mongoose and Ferret

383
00:16:20.913 --> 00:16:22.575
between 5:30 and 7:00.

384
00:16:22.648 --> 00:16:26.744
The barkeep vouches
for their presence.

385
00:16:26.819 --> 00:16:28.754
Jerome Bradley was at
the Mongoose and Ferret

386
00:16:28.821 --> 00:16:29.898
but left to go to the rink.

387
00:16:29.922 --> 00:16:31.891
Apparently. he'd
left his wallet there.

388
00:16:31.924 --> 00:16:34.894
- Mr. Bradley claims to
have found the body at 6:15.

389
00:16:34.927 --> 00:16:38.159
He could have arrived sooner.
so he remains on the list.

390
00:16:38.197 --> 00:16:39.221
- Fair enough.

391
00:16:39.265 --> 00:16:41.310
Now. Farrell claims that
he stayed after the practice

392
00:16:41.334 --> 00:16:42.393
to work on his wrist shot

393
00:16:42.435 --> 00:16:44.480
but that he was on his way
to the Mongoose and Ferret

394
00:16:44.504 --> 00:16:45.904
when the murder occurred.

395
00:16:45.938 --> 00:16:48.066
- No alibi. then.

396
00:16:48.107 --> 00:16:51.737
- Finally. Mr. Wallace was
on his way home at 6:00.

397
00:16:51.777 --> 00:16:53.712
but that's yet to be confirmed.

398
00:16:53.746 --> 00:16:55.078
- What about Driscoll?

399
00:16:55.114 --> 00:16:56.358
- He was supposed
to stop by. sir.

400
00:16:56.382 --> 00:16:58.374
to give an account of
his whereabouts. but...

401
00:16:58.417 --> 00:17:00.010
- Driscoll had a
dustup with the victim.

402
00:17:00.052 --> 00:17:01.384
He might be good for it.

403
00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:03.565
- Now. sirs. I know that
Eddie can seem full of himself.

404
00:17:03.589 --> 00:17:05.182
but I can vouch
for his character.

405
00:17:05.224 --> 00:17:07.420
- You keep your distance
from your pal. Crabtree.

406
00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:09.224
As long as Driscoll
remains a suspect.

407
00:17:09.262 --> 00:17:10.739
we can't risk giving
him any information

408
00:17:10.763 --> 00:17:12.425
that would compromise
this investigation.

409
00:17:12.465 --> 00:17:14.366
- Sir. I would never.

410
00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:16.960
- Just remember your
obligations as an officer of the law.

411
00:17:17.036 --> 00:17:18.197
Understood?

412
00:17:18.237 --> 00:17:20.035
- Sir.

413
00:17:22.909 --> 00:17:24.707
- What about Farrell?

414
00:17:24.744 --> 00:17:27.737
He was once a team forward.
right wing. if I'm not mistaken.

415
00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:29.373
- If you say so.

416
00:17:29.415 --> 00:17:31.543
- Well. when Simpson
was brought from Ottawa.

417
00:17:31.584 --> 00:17:33.382
Farrell had to give up his spot.

418
00:17:33.419 --> 00:17:35.859
That would have given him a
bone to pick with Simpson. surely.

419
00:17:35.888 --> 00:17:37.584
- Enough to kill him. sir?

420
00:17:37.623 --> 00:17:39.421
It's just a game.

421
00:17:39.458 --> 00:17:42.121
- You're not much interested
in sports. are you. Murdoch?

422
00:17:42.161 --> 00:17:44.255
If you were. you'd know
that there's no such thing

423
00:17:44.297 --> 00:17:46.732
as "just a game."

424
00:17:46.766 --> 00:17:49.600
- I had no idea you
were such an enthusiast.

425
00:17:49.635 --> 00:17:51.604
- It's not rugby. but it'll do.

426
00:17:51.637 --> 00:17:54.266
- I've always enjoyed
playing the game.

427
00:17:54.307 --> 00:17:57.835
but to sit and watch
the game being played

428
00:17:57.910 --> 00:17:59.139
seems like a waste of time.

429
00:17:59.178 --> 00:18:01.147
- It's a product of
our age. Murdoch.

430
00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:02.512
More leisure time.

431
00:18:02.582 --> 00:18:05.017
People looking for
ways to spend it.

432
00:18:05.084 --> 00:18:06.916
You should try it sometime.

433
00:18:08.955 --> 00:18:10.116
- Could read a book.

434
00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:18.930
- Mm.

435
00:18:20.433 --> 00:18:21.662
- [clears throat]

436
00:18:21.701 --> 00:18:23.431
Enjoy it while
you can. Driscoll.

437
00:18:23.469 --> 00:18:25.370
You'll soon be back
shoveling horse dung.

438
00:18:25.438 --> 00:18:26.770
- [laughing]

439
00:18:26.806 --> 00:18:28.536
Doubt that.

440
00:18:28.608 --> 00:18:30.338
I'm the future.

441
00:18:30.376 --> 00:18:33.005
- Eddie. we have to leave.

442
00:18:33.045 --> 00:18:37.540
- I'd love to buy a round
for the house before we go.

443
00:18:37.617 --> 00:18:39.347
Aw. you're an angel.

444
00:18:39.385 --> 00:18:40.385
- Mm-hmm.

445
00:18:42.388 --> 00:18:43.981
- One of the many perks

446
00:18:44.023 --> 00:18:46.049
of being the Wellington's
ace in the hole.

447
00:18:46.125 --> 00:18:47.855
- Eddie. you're
drunk as a skunk.

448
00:18:47.893 --> 00:18:48.937
- Let me buy you one.
- No. no. no.

449
00:18:48.961 --> 00:18:50.539
I'm not even supposed
to be talking to you.

450
00:18:50.563 --> 00:18:52.532
- Then why are you?
- Because I'm your friend.

451
00:18:52.565 --> 00:18:56.002
- So who does your
Detective Murdoch

452
00:18:56.035 --> 00:18:58.630
think committed the murder?

453
00:18:58.671 --> 00:18:59.798
[laughing]

454
00:18:59.839 --> 00:19:02.206
- Eddie. you were supposed...

455
00:19:02.241 --> 00:19:03.209
You were supposed to come in

456
00:19:03.242 --> 00:19:04.553
and give an account
of your whereabouts.

457
00:19:04.577 --> 00:19:07.046
- My whereabouts are
nobody else's business.

458
00:19:07.079 --> 00:19:08.079
- No one's business.

459
00:19:08.147 --> 00:19:10.673
Eddie. this is a murder
investigation. man.

460
00:19:10.716 --> 00:19:11.860
Now. when you met
me after that practice.

461
00:19:11.884 --> 00:19:12.884
you were late.

462
00:19:12.918 --> 00:19:14.079
Where were you?

463
00:19:14.153 --> 00:19:15.153
- You suspect me?

464
00:19:15.187 --> 00:19:16.553
- I don't...

465
00:19:17.556 --> 00:19:18.854
- Eddie. we have to go.

466
00:19:21.594 --> 00:19:24.257
- What's this? What about Lydia?

467
00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.890
- We're only young
once. Georgie boy.

468
00:19:26.932 --> 00:19:28.730
Time's fleeing!

469
00:19:28.768 --> 00:19:30.862
Have fun while we can.

470
00:19:30.903 --> 00:19:32.235
[chuckles]

471
00:19:36.609 --> 00:19:37.907
- Excuse me. gentlemen.

472
00:19:37.943 --> 00:19:39.377
A moment of your time?

473
00:19:39.412 --> 00:19:41.244
- We're busy.

474
00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:43.374
- Oh. it's you.

475
00:19:43.416 --> 00:19:46.079
This lanky chap's
a police officer.

476
00:19:46.118 --> 00:19:47.245
- Really?

477
00:19:47.286 --> 00:19:50.552
Well. I'm tremendously reassured
as to the safety of our streets.

478
00:19:52.725 --> 00:19:55.058
- You're Samuel Farrell.

479
00:19:55.094 --> 00:19:56.687
- I am indeed.

480
00:19:56.729 --> 00:19:58.960
- Do either you suspect
that anybody on the team

481
00:19:59.031 --> 00:20:00.761
might have a grudge
against Simpson?

482
00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:01.800
- His name was

483
00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:03.147
Mr. Archibald
Simpson. Constable.

484
00:20:03.202 --> 00:20:04.613
and he was as fine
a man as you'd meet.

485
00:20:04.637 --> 00:20:07.038
- A gentleman if ever
I've encountered one.

486
00:20:07.073 --> 00:20:09.269
He'd intended enlisting
after season's end

487
00:20:09.308 --> 00:20:12.369
to fight for our mother
country against the Boers.

488
00:20:12.411 --> 00:20:14.277
He was a highly
principled fellow.

489
00:20:14.313 --> 00:20:15.781
- Yes. unlike your lout friend.

490
00:20:15.815 --> 00:20:17.283
Do you want to know
who hated Archie?

491
00:20:17.316 --> 00:20:18.648
It was Eddie Driscoll.

492
00:20:18.718 --> 00:20:19.718
- Archie made it clear

493
00:20:19.752 --> 00:20:21.296
that Driscoll didn't belong
in a gentleman's game.

494
00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:22.720
- And Eddie killed him for it.

495
00:20:22.788 --> 00:20:23.812
Now. if you'll excuse me.

496
00:20:23.889 --> 00:20:25.551
I have an assignation
with a young lady.

497
00:20:29.929 --> 00:20:31.591
- Between you and me?

498
00:20:36.268 --> 00:20:37.668
I wanted Eddie on the team.

499
00:20:37.737 --> 00:20:40.502
He made us contenders.

500
00:20:40.573 --> 00:20:41.973
If you want someone
with a grudge.

501
00:20:42.007 --> 00:20:43.908
look no further than
our new captain.

502
00:20:43.943 --> 00:20:46.276
- Good night. barkeep.
Same time tomorrow.

503
00:20:46.312 --> 00:20:48.338
- Good night. Mr. Bradley.

504
00:20:48.414 --> 00:20:50.178
- Bradley?

505
00:20:50.249 --> 00:20:53.185
[ominous music]

506
00:20:53.252 --> 00:20:54.845
♪♪

507
00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.631
- We should have called
the wedding off sooner.

508
00:20:56.655 --> 00:20:59.090
but I was trying to
make everyone happy...

509
00:20:59.125 --> 00:21:00.991
Everyone except myself.

510
00:21:01.026 --> 00:21:02.824
- Well. I plan to win you back.

511
00:21:02.862 --> 00:21:05.161
- Jerome. you promised you
weren't going to bring that up.

512
00:21:05.197 --> 00:21:06.790
- And you promised
you'd marry me.

513
00:21:08.334 --> 00:21:10.667
So I suppose we're both liars.

514
00:21:18.177 --> 00:21:19.543
You're coming back to me.

515
00:21:19.612 --> 00:21:21.171
It's just a matter of time.

516
00:21:21.213 --> 00:21:22.841
You don't have a
choice in the matter.

517
00:21:22.882 --> 00:21:26.978
I'll win the Cup. and then
I'm gonna win you back.

518
00:21:27.019 --> 00:21:28.612
Now. shall we order desert?

519
00:21:28.654 --> 00:21:31.055
- I really should
be going. Jerome.

520
00:21:31.123 --> 00:21:32.489
I rise early.

521
00:21:34.627 --> 00:21:36.357
Thank you for a lovely dinner.

522
00:21:36.996 --> 00:21:37.996
Good-bye.

523
00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:39.464
- Good-bye? I
don't think so. Emily.

524
00:21:39.498 --> 00:21:40.709
I'll be seeing you
sooner than you think.

525
00:21:40.733 --> 00:21:42.702
- Good-bye. Jerome.

526
00:21:46.305 --> 00:21:47.637
- [clears throat]
[snaps fingers]

527
00:21:48.674 --> 00:21:50.336
- Jerome Bradley?

528
00:21:50.376 --> 00:21:52.811
- Sir. when the Wellingtons'
first captain retired last year.

529
00:21:52.845 --> 00:21:54.507
Bradley was set
to become captain.

530
00:21:54.547 --> 00:21:56.539
Then they brought
Simpson in from Ottawa.

531
00:21:56.582 --> 00:21:59.017
Simpson insisted that
he become captain.

532
00:21:59.051 --> 00:22:01.316
and Wallace capitulated.

533
00:22:01.353 --> 00:22:04.187
- Ah. that must have
made Mr. Bradley upset.

534
00:22:04.223 --> 00:22:05.919
- So upset that he quit. sir.

535
00:22:05.991 --> 00:22:07.254
He only rejoined last month

536
00:22:07.326 --> 00:22:09.852
when he found out the team
was gonna challenge for the Cup.

537
00:22:11.063 --> 00:22:14.192
- But still. that's hardly
a reason to kill Simpson.

538
00:22:14.233 --> 00:22:15.343
- Well. think about it. sir.

539
00:22:15.367 --> 00:22:17.393
If Jerome Bradley was
captain and they won.

540
00:22:17.436 --> 00:22:19.564
he would be the first
ever Ontario athlete

541
00:22:19.605 --> 00:22:21.574
to hoist Lord Stanley's mug.

542
00:22:23.275 --> 00:22:26.439
And. sir. he's had a few
run-ins with the Constabulary.

543
00:22:26.512 --> 00:22:27.605
No convictions. of course.

544
00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:29.391
His high-priced lawyers
have to seen to that.

545
00:22:29.415 --> 00:22:30.678
needless to say.

546
00:22:32.585 --> 00:22:35.783
- George. you seem to
have it in for this Bradley.

547
00:22:35.855 --> 00:22:38.222
- No. sir. I don't.

548
00:22:38.257 --> 00:22:40.283
Well. perhaps I do.

549
00:22:40.359 --> 00:22:43.022
I know he's well-bred and
from a good family and all that.

550
00:22:43.062 --> 00:22:44.121
but he's...

551
00:22:44.196 --> 00:22:46.427
He sort of lords it
over you. doesn't he?

552
00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:52.563
- Bring him in.

553
00:22:55.875 --> 00:22:57.400
- What's this?

554
00:22:57.443 --> 00:22:58.843
- It would appear
you have a history

555
00:22:58.878 --> 00:23:00.312
of violence. Mr. Bradley.

556
00:23:00.379 --> 00:23:01.756
- I have a history of
getting into punch-ups

557
00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:02.804
when I've had a few.

558
00:23:02.882 --> 00:23:05.545
- I would like to note that my
client has never been convicted.

559
00:23:05.584 --> 00:23:06.628
- You have the nerve to suggest

560
00:23:06.652 --> 00:23:09.053
that this predisposes
me to murder?

561
00:23:09.088 --> 00:23:11.148
- Did you or did
you not quit the team

562
00:23:11.223 --> 00:23:12.714
when Mr. Simpson became captain?

563
00:23:12.758 --> 00:23:14.590
- I quit because I
could not stand by

564
00:23:14.627 --> 00:23:15.788
and watch the team I loved

565
00:23:15.828 --> 00:23:18.059
sullied by the likes
of Eddie Driscoll.

566
00:23:18.964 --> 00:23:21.263
Archie felt the same way.

567
00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:23.180
He and Mr. Wallace
almost came to blows over it.

568
00:23:24.303 --> 00:23:25.635
- When was this?

569
00:23:25.671 --> 00:23:28.266
- Last week in
Mr. Wallace's office.

570
00:23:28.307 --> 00:23:29.985
I don't know what they
were shouting about.

571
00:23:30.009 --> 00:23:33.343
but I heard Eddie's
name loud and clear.

572
00:23:33.412 --> 00:23:34.778
If anyone killed Archie.

573
00:23:34.813 --> 00:23:37.476
it was probably that
low-class farm boy.

574
00:23:46.792 --> 00:23:48.090
You call yourself a lawyer?

575
00:23:48.127 --> 00:23:50.289
Then why do I have
to do all the talking?

576
00:23:52.665 --> 00:23:55.658
- Bloody toff. I
wish he was guilty.

577
00:23:55.701 --> 00:23:57.381
- I'd like to know what
Simpson and Wallace

578
00:23:57.436 --> 00:23:58.961
were having words about.

579
00:23:59.004 --> 00:24:00.768
- Tread lightly. Murdoch.

580
00:24:00.806 --> 00:24:03.674
or Giles will be all
over us like a rash.

581
00:24:03.709 --> 00:24:07.271
- Sir. Chief Constable
Giles is an honest policeman.

582
00:24:07.313 --> 00:24:10.511
If he holds any malice for us.
it's because we've earned it.

583
00:24:15.487 --> 00:24:17.820
What were you and
Mr. Simpson arguing about?

584
00:24:17.856 --> 00:24:19.017
- Strategy.

585
00:24:19.058 --> 00:24:21.960
Proper deployment of players.

586
00:24:21.994 --> 00:24:24.122
- Specifically.

587
00:24:24.163 --> 00:24:25.495
- Simpson was unhappy

588
00:24:25.531 --> 00:24:29.730
with Eddie Driscoll's
place on the team.

589
00:24:29.802 --> 00:24:32.362
as were a number of players.

590
00:24:32.404 --> 00:24:33.997
- And why was that?

591
00:24:34.039 --> 00:24:36.235
By all accounts. he was
your most talented player.

592
00:24:36.308 --> 00:24:39.676
- Oh. yes. he was that.
but he wasn't one of them.

593
00:24:39.712 --> 00:24:42.375
If I could speak frankly.

594
00:24:42.414 --> 00:24:46.818
most of the players on the team
thought Driscoll beneath them.

595
00:24:46.852 --> 00:24:50.584
They only tolerated him
because of his abilities.

596
00:24:50.656 --> 00:24:53.148
- And your captain
shared that view?

597
00:24:53.192 --> 00:24:58.062
- No. Simpson didn't care
about Driscoll's background.

598
00:24:58.097 --> 00:25:01.659
He was somewhat of a
republican in that sense.

599
00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:03.498
He disliked Driscoll
because of the manner

600
00:25:03.535 --> 00:25:06.164
in which he played the game.

601
00:25:06.205 --> 00:25:08.003
- And he wanted
him off the team?

602
00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:10.168
- Well. that's what we
were arguing about.

603
00:25:10.209 --> 00:25:11.700
I told him it was
none of his business

604
00:25:11.744 --> 00:25:14.543
to be issuing me ultimatums.

605
00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:18.517
But Simpson said it
was either him or Eddie.

606
00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:22.351
- Did Eddie Driscoll know that
Simpson wanted him dropped?

607
00:25:22.388 --> 00:25:25.552
- Well. he certainly
didn't keep it a secret.

608
00:25:25.591 --> 00:25:29.119
Now. I'm due for
my supper. Detective.

609
00:25:29.194 --> 00:25:31.629
Can you let yourself out?
- Yes. of course.

610
00:25:31.697 --> 00:25:32.697
- Good.

611
00:26:00.626 --> 00:26:02.925
- Aha.

612
00:26:02.961 --> 00:26:06.295
George. bring in...

613
00:26:06.331 --> 00:26:07.731
Samuel Farrell.

614
00:26:10.669 --> 00:26:14.436
- I wasn't in Mr. Wallace's
office that day.

615
00:26:14.473 --> 00:26:17.637
- Then how do you explain
this piece of your stick

616
00:26:17.676 --> 00:26:20.510
that we found on the floor
in Mr. Wallace's office?

617
00:26:20.579 --> 00:26:22.844
- I have no idea.

618
00:26:22.915 --> 00:26:25.441
Anybody could have grabbed
it. either by mistake or by intent.

619
00:26:27.453 --> 00:26:30.116
- Where were you at the
time of the murder. Mr. Farrell?

620
00:26:30.155 --> 00:26:31.987
- As I recall. I stayed
late after practice.

621
00:26:32.024 --> 00:26:35.426
I went for a walk
and then to the tavern.

622
00:26:35.461 --> 00:26:38.260
- Do you always take a
walk after a hard practice?

623
00:26:38.297 --> 00:26:40.596
- A stroll cools me down.

624
00:26:42.034 --> 00:26:43.127
- Did anyone see you?

625
00:26:43.168 --> 00:26:45.000
- I have no idea.

626
00:26:45.037 --> 00:26:48.599
- So you have no alibi
for the time of the murder?

627
00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.973
- All you have is a shard
of my stick. Detective.

628
00:26:51.009 --> 00:26:54.377
I don't see that
I need an alibi.

629
00:26:54.446 --> 00:26:56.608
So if you'll excuse me.

630
00:26:56.648 --> 00:26:58.640
- Not so fast. Mr. Farrell.

631
00:26:58.684 --> 00:27:00.095
You'll have plenty
of time to remember

632
00:27:00.119 --> 00:27:01.529
who saw you strolling
when you're cooling off

633
00:27:01.553 --> 00:27:03.112
in one of our cells.

634
00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:06.520
- Mr. Farrell. how tall are you?

635
00:27:06.558 --> 00:27:09.050
- What's it to you?
- Answer him.

636
00:27:09.128 --> 00:27:10.824
- I'm 5'8".

637
00:27:10.863 --> 00:27:14.027
My low center of gravity
gives me an advantage.

638
00:27:14.066 --> 00:27:16.865
- Hodge. escort
Mr. Farrell to the cells.

639
00:27:20.339 --> 00:27:22.069
What were you thinking?

640
00:27:22.141 --> 00:27:25.873
- Sir. Mr. Farrell is the
shortest player on his team.

641
00:27:25.911 --> 00:27:28.073
If I can determine the
height of the killer...

642
00:27:28.147 --> 00:27:29.911
- It can prove it was him.

643
00:27:54.439 --> 00:27:55.737
- Right here. ma'am.

644
00:27:59.912 --> 00:28:02.211
- George Crabtree.

645
00:28:02.247 --> 00:28:03.613
- Lydia.

646
00:28:03.682 --> 00:28:05.844
- Hello. George.
It's lovely to see you.

647
00:28:05.884 --> 00:28:07.182
- Well. you as well.

648
00:28:07.219 --> 00:28:08.744
What brings you here?

649
00:28:08.787 --> 00:28:09.831
- I was asked to
make a statement

650
00:28:09.855 --> 00:28:11.790
as to Eddie's whereabouts
at a particular time.

651
00:28:11.857 --> 00:28:13.120
which I've now done.

652
00:28:13.192 --> 00:28:15.127
- Of course. So he was with you?

653
00:28:15.194 --> 00:28:16.389
- That's correct.

654
00:28:16.428 --> 00:28:17.623
Immediately after practice.

655
00:28:17.696 --> 00:28:19.927
right when poor
Mr. Simpson was killed.

656
00:28:22.267 --> 00:28:24.896
- Right. well. thank
you for coming in.

657
00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.100
Lydia. sit down.

658
00:28:30.142 --> 00:28:31.269
- Thank you.

659
00:28:33.712 --> 00:28:35.613
I know you and Eddie have
been chumming around.

660
00:28:35.647 --> 00:28:37.479
- Well. I've seen him.

661
00:28:37.549 --> 00:28:40.417
- George. is he still
being true to me?

662
00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:43.479
- As far as I can tell.

663
00:28:43.555 --> 00:28:46.320
he's still the same
old Eddie Driscoll.

664
00:28:46.391 --> 00:28:48.724
- So you haven't seen anything?

665
00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:52.561
- Well. all I've seen. Lydia.
is that he has many admirers.

666
00:28:52.598 --> 00:28:54.396
- He's always had that.

667
00:28:54.433 --> 00:28:55.662
- Mm.

668
00:28:55.734 --> 00:28:58.966
- Well. look at you.
big city policeman.

669
00:29:00.439 --> 00:29:01.839
An important man.

670
00:29:01.907 --> 00:29:03.466
- Important. I don't know...

671
00:29:03.508 --> 00:29:07.001
- George. I need you
to get me six melons.

672
00:29:07.079 --> 00:29:09.344
- Uh. sir. they're
out of season.

673
00:29:09.414 --> 00:29:12.077
- Right. Six pumpkins. then.

674
00:29:12.117 --> 00:29:13.346
- Yes. sir.

675
00:29:15.287 --> 00:29:19.088
Some of my tasks are
more menial than others.

676
00:29:21.426 --> 00:29:22.758
- Thank you. George.

677
00:29:25.697 --> 00:29:26.790
Right. then.

678
00:29:31.169 --> 00:29:33.832
- The angle of impact
is too high on the skull.

679
00:29:39.011 --> 00:29:40.309
Still too high.

680
00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:43.608
Perfect.

681
00:29:43.649 --> 00:29:46.175
Now. given the difference
between your height

682
00:29:46.218 --> 00:29:47.846
and the height of this pumpkin.

683
00:29:47.886 --> 00:29:49.946
and subtracting that
from Mr. Simpson's height.

684
00:29:49.988 --> 00:29:53.789
we can conclude that
the killer is 5'10" tall.

685
00:29:53.825 --> 00:29:55.191
- Or maybe he
was taller than that

686
00:29:55.227 --> 00:29:57.389
but crouching when he attacked.

687
00:29:57.462 --> 00:29:59.158
- That's a good point. George.

688
00:29:59.197 --> 00:30:01.063
We can't determine
the maximum height;

689
00:30:01.133 --> 00:30:02.624
only the minimum.

690
00:30:02.668 --> 00:30:05.570
- And if the killer
was at least 5'10"...

691
00:30:05.637 --> 00:30:08.505
- That means Samuel
Farrell can't be our killer.

692
00:30:10.509 --> 00:30:12.239
- Bloody hell. I
was sure it was him.

693
00:30:12.311 --> 00:30:14.507
No alibi. It was
his hockey stick.

694
00:30:14.546 --> 00:30:15.556
- Well. at the very least. sir.

695
00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:18.573
this narrows our
list of suspects.

696
00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:19.811
- So what next?

697
00:30:19.885 --> 00:30:22.650
- I still think he was
killed in Wallace's office.

698
00:30:22.688 --> 00:30:25.021
- Do you think Wallace did it?
- Not necessarily.

699
00:30:25.057 --> 00:30:27.652
Mr. Wallace kept his
office door unlocked.

700
00:30:27.693 --> 00:30:29.594
It does raise the
question. however:

701
00:30:29.661 --> 00:30:32.324
what was Simpson
doing in Wallace's office?

702
00:30:32.364 --> 00:30:33.923
What have you. George?

703
00:30:33.999 --> 00:30:35.677
- Sir. I was going
through Simpson's effects

704
00:30:35.701 --> 00:30:36.669
as you asked.

705
00:30:36.702 --> 00:30:39.228
Amongst the other
paraphernalia was this:

706
00:30:39.271 --> 00:30:41.035
a return train ticket to Ottawa

707
00:30:41.073 --> 00:30:43.269
dated the 29th and
30th of November.

708
00:30:43.342 --> 00:30:45.368
- That's only a few days ago.

709
00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:47.345
- Why would he go up to Ottawa?

710
00:30:47.379 --> 00:30:48.523
- Well. I don't know. sir.
but there's something else

711
00:30:48.547 --> 00:30:50.345
written on the back of it there.

712
00:30:50.382 --> 00:30:52.283
- J.R.R.

713
00:30:52.351 --> 00:30:53.717
- John Ross Robertson?

714
00:30:55.087 --> 00:30:57.352
- Why. yes. very good. sir.

715
00:30:57.389 --> 00:31:01.292
Perhaps 221 is his
Parliament Hill office number.

716
00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:04.639
- Looks like you're going
to Ottawa. me old mucker.

717
00:31:04.663 --> 00:31:06.859
[mysterious music]

718
00:31:06.898 --> 00:31:09.424
- Yes. Simpson did make
an appointment to see me.

719
00:31:09.468 --> 00:31:11.869
He lodged a very
troubling complaint.

720
00:31:11.903 --> 00:31:13.303
- And that was?

721
00:31:13.372 --> 00:31:15.307
- Mr. Simpson maintained
that Langston Wallace

722
00:31:15.374 --> 00:31:18.902
was paying one of his
players for his services.

723
00:31:18.944 --> 00:31:21.470
- What would the consequence
be if that proved true?

724
00:31:21.546 --> 00:31:23.139
- Immediate and
permanent ejection

725
00:31:23.215 --> 00:31:25.480
from the Ontario
Hockey Association.

726
00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:26.916
- I see.

727
00:31:26.952 --> 00:31:29.751
What was your reaction to
Mr. Simpson's allegation?

728
00:31:29.788 --> 00:31:32.087
- I told him that if he wanted
an investigation launched.

729
00:31:32.124 --> 00:31:34.889
he would need to provide
some hard evidence.

730
00:31:34.926 --> 00:31:37.486
- Had he any?
- Nothing but hearsay.

731
00:31:37.562 --> 00:31:39.428
While I knew Simpson
to be an honest man.

732
00:31:39.464 --> 00:31:41.057
an investigation at this point

733
00:31:41.099 --> 00:31:42.761
would inevitably
have forestalled

734
00:31:42.801 --> 00:31:44.565
the Wellingtons'
challenge for the Cup.

735
00:31:44.603 --> 00:31:46.936
for which there's been
so much anticipation.

736
00:31:46.972 --> 00:31:50.067
- Yes. So you did
nothing further?

737
00:31:50.108 --> 00:31:52.976
- I made some
discreet inquiries.

738
00:31:53.011 --> 00:31:55.947
- Did you find any basis for
Mr. Simpson's suspicions?

739
00:31:55.981 --> 00:32:00.248
- I found no cause to
pursue the matter further.

740
00:32:00.285 --> 00:32:01.981
- Did he mention the
name of the player

741
00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:04.580
he suspected was being paid?

742
00:32:04.623 --> 00:32:05.989
- He did.

743
00:32:06.024 --> 00:32:07.754
- And?

744
00:32:07.793 --> 00:32:09.421
- With all due
respect. Detective.

745
00:32:09.461 --> 00:32:10.690
a man's good name

746
00:32:10.762 --> 00:32:12.856
and the reputation
of a fine hockey team

747
00:32:12.931 --> 00:32:15.298
is on the line here.

748
00:32:15.333 --> 00:32:18.428
- I assure you. I will handle
all aspects of this conversation

749
00:32:18.470 --> 00:32:21.372
with the utmost discretion.

750
00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:24.376
- This game is being taken
over by rogues and capitalists.

751
00:32:24.443 --> 00:32:26.469
Detective Murdoch.

752
00:32:26.511 --> 00:32:29.709
I'm doing all in
my power to stop it.

753
00:32:29.781 --> 00:32:32.717
I do hope you are on side.

754
00:32:32.784 --> 00:32:34.480
- Sir.

755
00:32:34.519 --> 00:32:35.817
The name.

756
00:32:38.123 --> 00:32:40.683
- Eddie Driscoll.

757
00:32:42.894 --> 00:32:44.453
- Eddie Driscoll is being paid?

758
00:32:44.496 --> 00:32:45.964
- That's what
Mr. Simpson believed.

759
00:32:45.997 --> 00:32:47.625
but he didn't have the proof.

760
00:32:47.666 --> 00:32:49.677
- Perhaps that's why
Simpson was in Wallace's office

761
00:32:49.701 --> 00:32:50.964
the night he was killed.

762
00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:54.215
- Well. sir. if he did find
the proof he was looking for.

763
00:32:54.239 --> 00:32:55.550
that would certainly
spell the end

764
00:32:55.574 --> 00:32:57.805
of Wallace's grand
ambitions for his team.

765
00:32:57.843 --> 00:32:59.243
- Well. forget the Cup.

766
00:32:59.311 --> 00:33:01.511
This would get the Wellingtons
kicked out of the league.

767
00:33:01.546 --> 00:33:04.482
- Still. sir. it seems
absurd that an individual

768
00:33:04.516 --> 00:33:07.145
would actually pay
others to play a game.

769
00:33:07.185 --> 00:33:09.051
- It's more and more
commonplace. Murdoch.

770
00:33:09.087 --> 00:33:11.420
It's even happening with
the football back home.

771
00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:12.583
Oh. bloody hell.

772
00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:16.492
- Chief Constable.

773
00:33:16.528 --> 00:33:19.396
- I just had a message
from Langston Wallace.

774
00:33:19.431 --> 00:33:21.923
You're investigating a
rumor that the Wellingtons

775
00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:24.663
have been employing
a paid professional?

776
00:33:24.703 --> 00:33:25.762
- We are. sir.

777
00:33:25.837 --> 00:33:27.215
- But you must know
what that means.

778
00:33:27.239 --> 00:33:28.867
It would be the end of the team.

779
00:33:28.907 --> 00:33:30.535
- Sir. I'm only looking into it

780
00:33:30.575 --> 00:33:32.407
as it pertains to
our investigation.

781
00:33:32.444 --> 00:33:34.936
Should Langston Wallace
prove to be innocent of murder.

782
00:33:35.013 --> 00:33:36.675
I see no reason
why the allegations

783
00:33:36.715 --> 00:33:38.707
should come to light.

784
00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:40.082
- No.

785
00:33:40.118 --> 00:33:43.714
No. if it's true. it must
be brought to light.

786
00:33:43.755 --> 00:33:44.848
- Sir?

787
00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:46.367
- There's no greater
stain on the face of hockey

788
00:33:46.391 --> 00:33:48.883
than the specter
of professionalism.

789
00:33:48.927 --> 00:33:50.071
- Greater than
cold-blooded murder?

790
00:33:50.095 --> 00:33:52.257
- Yes. I admit that
Langston Wallace

791
00:33:52.297 --> 00:33:53.458
has a strong motivation

792
00:33:53.532 --> 00:33:56.127
to try to keep this
from getting out.

793
00:33:56.201 --> 00:33:58.727
Perhaps at the expense
of a young man's life.

794
00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:01.296
Get to the bottom
of it. Murdoch.

795
00:34:01.373 --> 00:34:04.241
Langston may be my friend.
but if he's broken the law...

796
00:34:04.276 --> 00:34:05.869
- Yes. sir. I will.

797
00:34:09.147 --> 00:34:10.291
- You see. this
is the difference

798
00:34:10.315 --> 00:34:12.477
between us. gentlemen.

799
00:34:12.551 --> 00:34:15.385
I don't let my personal
friendships come between me

800
00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:17.548
and my sworn obligations.

801
00:34:23.328 --> 00:34:26.264
- I've been looking at
your ledger. Mr. Wallace.

802
00:34:26.298 --> 00:34:27.994
You make a significant
amount of money

803
00:34:28.066 --> 00:34:31.002
charging admission to
Wellingtons hockey games.

804
00:34:31.069 --> 00:34:32.594
- I do all right.

805
00:34:32.637 --> 00:34:33.832
- You would make even more

806
00:34:33.905 --> 00:34:36.898
if you were the owner
of a championship team.

807
00:34:36.942 --> 00:34:40.504
Acquiring Eddie Driscoll
made that a very real possibility.

808
00:34:40.579 --> 00:34:43.947
- I'm already a
wealthy man. Murdoch.

809
00:34:43.982 --> 00:34:46.281
I certainly am not in
need of the gate recipes

810
00:34:46.318 --> 00:34:48.150
the team produces.

811
00:34:48.186 --> 00:34:51.281
- You deny paying
Eddie Driscoll. then.

812
00:34:51.323 --> 00:34:52.291
- Of course I deny it.

813
00:34:52.324 --> 00:34:54.520
Professionalism would
be the end of hockey.

814
00:34:54.593 --> 00:34:56.960
The gate recipes would all
end up going to the players

815
00:34:56.995 --> 00:35:00.989
as teams tried to
outbid each other.

816
00:35:01.032 --> 00:35:03.797
- Tell me. how does a
man like Eddie Driscoll

817
00:35:03.835 --> 00:35:05.463
pay his room and board?

818
00:35:05.503 --> 00:35:06.971
He has no job.

819
00:35:07.005 --> 00:35:09.201
but he seems to have
plenty of money to spend.

820
00:35:09.274 --> 00:35:12.301
- It's not my concern.

821
00:35:12.344 --> 00:35:15.872
- Then why does your
ledger list a $5 debit

822
00:35:15.947 --> 00:35:18.314
for miscellaneous
expenses for every game?

823
00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:20.649
- Because I have
miscellaneous expenses.

824
00:35:20.685 --> 00:35:22.881
- Of exactly $5.

825
00:35:22.954 --> 00:35:24.445
every game.

826
00:35:24.489 --> 00:35:27.288
and only since Eddie
Driscoll began playing

827
00:35:27.325 --> 00:35:30.318
for the Wellingtons?

828
00:35:30.362 --> 00:35:33.332
According to the rules of the
Ontario Hockey Association.

829
00:35:33.365 --> 00:35:36.460
payment of a player
is strictly forbidden.

830
00:35:36.501 --> 00:35:37.662
If it were to come out

831
00:35:37.702 --> 00:35:39.146
that you have been
paying Eddie Driscoll.

832
00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:41.799
you would forfeit your
chances at the Stanley trophy.

833
00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:44.139
- Cup. Murdoch. It's a cup.

834
00:35:44.175 --> 00:35:46.974
- Cup.

835
00:35:47.012 --> 00:35:50.244
I believe Simpson
stumbled upon this ledger

836
00:35:50.315 --> 00:35:52.648
and realized it was
the proof he needed.

837
00:35:52.684 --> 00:35:54.482
and you killed him for it.

838
00:35:55.854 --> 00:35:58.756
- Look at me. Murdoch.

839
00:35:58.823 --> 00:36:00.985
Do you really think
that a man my age

840
00:36:01.026 --> 00:36:03.359
is capable of killing
a young sportsman

841
00:36:03.395 --> 00:36:05.023
in the prime of his life?

842
00:36:14.906 --> 00:36:16.517
- So if it's not Wallace.
that just leaves

843
00:36:16.541 --> 00:36:18.567
Driscoll and
Bradley as suspects.

844
00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:20.545
- But Eddie
Driscoll had an alibi

845
00:36:20.578 --> 00:36:22.012
confirmed by his wife.

846
00:36:23.615 --> 00:36:24.742
- Sirs...

847
00:36:24.783 --> 00:36:26.376
- What is it?

848
00:36:26.418 --> 00:36:29.217
- Oh. I wouldn't pull much
truck in Lydia Driscoll's words.

849
00:36:29.254 --> 00:36:33.214
I'm quite sure Eddie
coerced her to lie for him.

850
00:36:33.258 --> 00:36:34.886
- Damn it. Crabtree.
- George.

851
00:36:34.926 --> 00:36:36.237
- Why didn't you
mention that earlier?

852
00:36:36.261 --> 00:36:38.753
- Because I think he's an
adulterer. not a murderer.

853
00:36:38.797 --> 00:36:40.356
And I think he had
his wife lie for him

854
00:36:40.398 --> 00:36:41.764
because he couldn't get an alibi

855
00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:43.234
from the woman
he was actually with.

856
00:36:43.268 --> 00:36:44.361
- Bloody hell. Crabtree.

857
00:36:44.402 --> 00:36:46.132
- He wouldn't have
killed anybody. sir.

858
00:36:46.204 --> 00:36:47.399
I'm sure of that.

859
00:36:47.439 --> 00:36:49.431
- Bring him in.

860
00:36:49.474 --> 00:36:52.569
- No. sir. Bring
the wife in first.

861
00:36:54.279 --> 00:36:55.747
- I wasn't lying.

862
00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:59.239
My husband was
with me after practice.

863
00:36:59.284 --> 00:37:01.776
- He came all the way
home after practice

864
00:37:01.820 --> 00:37:07.487
and then went the same distance
all the way back to the tavern?

865
00:37:07.559 --> 00:37:09.221
- He was being a good husband.

866
00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:10.751
What's wrong with that?

867
00:37:14.165 --> 00:37:15.724
Why don't you believe me?

868
00:37:27.412 --> 00:37:30.473
- You were right.
George. She was lying.

869
00:37:30.515 --> 00:37:33.246
Arrest Eddie Driscoll.

870
00:37:33.284 --> 00:37:34.912
- Sir.

871
00:37:37.856 --> 00:37:41.326
- [laughing]

872
00:37:41.359 --> 00:37:44.420
- Eddie. I've been
sent to arrest you.

873
00:37:44.462 --> 00:37:45.521
- Again?

874
00:37:45.597 --> 00:37:47.361
- Just come with me.

875
00:37:50.135 --> 00:37:52.934
- Eddie Driscoll. who is this.

876
00:37:52.971 --> 00:37:54.667
this floozy you're with?

877
00:37:54.706 --> 00:37:56.766
- You dare call me a floozy?

878
00:37:56.808 --> 00:37:58.640
- I—I'm not with anybody.

879
00:37:59.444 --> 00:38:01.106
- You were with
her. weren't you?

880
00:38:03.314 --> 00:38:04.839
You bastard.

881
00:38:04.883 --> 00:38:06.647
You made me lie for you.

882
00:38:08.186 --> 00:38:10.519
And to you. George
Crabtree. of all people.

883
00:38:10.555 --> 00:38:13.218
I thought you were my friend.

884
00:38:13.291 --> 00:38:16.693
I ask you directly. and you
as good as lied to my face.

885
00:38:16.728 --> 00:38:19.129
- Lydia— - You
should be ashamed.

886
00:38:24.669 --> 00:38:26.399
- Did you bring
her here. George?

887
00:38:26.471 --> 00:38:28.064
- No. I didn't bring
her here. Eddie.

888
00:38:28.139 --> 00:38:30.472
She must have followed
me from the station.

889
00:38:31.376 --> 00:38:34.210
She is a good woman.
and she lied for you.

890
00:38:34.245 --> 00:38:35.907
And I tried to believe her.

891
00:38:37.849 --> 00:38:39.393
You better have a good
story as to where you were

892
00:38:39.417 --> 00:38:41.010
when Archie Simpson was killed.

893
00:38:47.192 --> 00:38:49.058
- I can't. Eddie. You know that.

894
00:38:55.433 --> 00:38:57.095
- Unfortunately for
you. Mr. Driscoll.

895
00:38:57.168 --> 00:38:59.694
your alibi no longer holds.

896
00:39:00.772 --> 00:39:03.765
Your wife has
retracted her statement.

897
00:39:06.010 --> 00:39:10.243
I think that Mr. Wallace
had been paying you.

898
00:39:10.281 --> 00:39:13.183
Mr. Simpson found out about
it. and you murdered him.

899
00:39:13.218 --> 00:39:14.709
- You're wrong.

900
00:39:14.752 --> 00:39:16.230
I don't care who
knew I was being paid.

901
00:39:16.254 --> 00:39:18.621
If I don't play for this
team. I can play for another.

902
00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:20.591
They pay people in America.

903
00:39:22.026 --> 00:39:23.358
Better money than I make here.

904
00:39:24.462 --> 00:39:26.226
- All right.

905
00:39:26.264 --> 00:39:28.199
Where were you the
evening of the murder?

906
00:39:28.233 --> 00:39:30.134
- I was with a woman.

907
00:39:30.201 --> 00:39:32.067
- Her name?
- A gentleman doesn't...

908
00:39:32.103 --> 00:39:34.197
- Her name. sir.

909
00:39:36.875 --> 00:39:39.777
- Felicity Wallace.

910
00:39:39.811 --> 00:39:41.939
- Langston Wallace's
granddaughter.

911
00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:44.211
- But she won't admit
to being with me.

912
00:39:44.249 --> 00:39:46.115
- What time did you leave her?

913
00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:47.812
- I don't remember.

914
00:39:47.886 --> 00:39:50.117
- Where did you go after your...

915
00:39:50.154 --> 00:39:51.486
assignation?

916
00:39:51.556 --> 00:39:53.718
- To the Mongoose and
Ferret. where I met George.

917
00:39:53.758 --> 00:39:59.288
- Constable Crabtree stated
that you met him at 6:15.

918
00:39:59.330 --> 00:40:01.799
That would have given you 15
minutes to commit the murder

919
00:40:01.833 --> 00:40:03.563
and make it back to the tavern.

920
00:40:07.906 --> 00:40:11.934
- I was at a bookmaker's
shop before I met George.

921
00:40:11.976 --> 00:40:13.604
- For what reason?

922
00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:17.977
- I was betting on myself
to take my team to victory

923
00:40:18.016 --> 00:40:20.485
against the Shamrocks.

924
00:40:20.518 --> 00:40:23.249
My lady friend fronted
me the $50 I was to receive

925
00:40:23.288 --> 00:40:26.349
as a bonus if the
Wellingtons won the Cup.

926
00:40:26.424 --> 00:40:28.154
The Shamrocks may
be heavy favorites.

927
00:40:28.192 --> 00:40:31.356
but the bookmakers know
nothing about what I can do.

928
00:40:31.429 --> 00:40:35.423
My $50 will soon net me $500.

929
00:40:36.634 --> 00:40:40.594
- I care neither about your
game or your wagering.

930
00:40:40.638 --> 00:40:42.607
I'm looking for a murderer.

931
00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:46.008
- I didn't kill Simpson.
and that's the truth.

932
00:41:00.458 --> 00:41:01.619
- [gasps]

933
00:41:03.561 --> 00:41:04.995
- Hello. my darling.

934
00:41:05.029 --> 00:41:07.157
- Jerome. you scared
the daylights out of me.

935
00:41:07.198 --> 00:41:09.895
- Oh. I'm so sorry. my dearest.

936
00:41:09.968 --> 00:41:10.901
Far from my intention.

937
00:41:10.969 --> 00:41:13.336
- You're drunk.

938
00:41:13.371 --> 00:41:14.395
- What of it?

939
00:41:14.472 --> 00:41:15.633
- You can't be here. Jerome.

940
00:41:15.673 --> 00:41:17.005
You must leave immediately.

941
00:41:17.041 --> 00:41:18.052
or I'll have
Constable Crabtree...

942
00:41:18.076 --> 00:41:20.307
- Oh. Constable Crabtree!

943
00:41:20.345 --> 00:41:21.574
Come and save you.

944
00:41:21.646 --> 00:41:23.046
[laughing]

945
00:41:23.081 --> 00:41:24.743
That's a joke if
I've ever heard one.

946
00:41:24.816 --> 00:41:27.411
Now. into my arms. my dearest.

947
00:41:27.485 --> 00:41:29.386
- This behavior is
unacceptable. Jerome.

948
00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:30.718
- Unacceptable?

949
00:41:30.755 --> 00:41:32.132
You have the nerve
to say that to me?

950
00:41:32.156 --> 00:41:35.149
I offered to bring a
lowly working-class girl

951
00:41:35.193 --> 00:41:36.491
up to my station.

952
00:41:36.527 --> 00:41:37.859
- I have apologized for that.

953
00:41:37.895 --> 00:41:39.557
There's nothing
further for me to do.

954
00:41:39.597 --> 00:41:41.498
- You can reverse
that earlier error.

955
00:41:41.532 --> 00:41:43.694
- It wasn't an error.

956
00:41:43.735 --> 00:41:46.068
The mistake would have been
to go through with the wedding.

957
00:41:47.505 --> 00:41:51.670
- You ungrateful whore.

958
00:41:51.709 --> 00:41:52.904
- Ah!

959
00:41:53.845 --> 00:41:55.656
Take your leave. sir.
or I'll have to arrest you.

960
00:41:55.680 --> 00:41:58.013
- This is none of your
business. Now leave us to it.

961
00:41:58.049 --> 00:41:59.449
- Get out of here!

962
00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:05.613
- [grunting]

963
00:42:14.732 --> 00:42:16.598
- Get up!

964
00:42:16.634 --> 00:42:19.866
You're under arrest for
assaulting a police officer

965
00:42:19.904 --> 00:42:21.736
and a lady.

966
00:42:21.773 --> 00:42:23.537
I apologize for the
mess. Dr. Grace.

967
00:42:32.884 --> 00:42:33.977
Good morning. sir.

968
00:42:35.286 --> 00:42:36.811
Eddie Driscoll's
alibi checks out.

969
00:42:36.888 --> 00:42:39.289
He laid the bet at exactly 6:00.

970
00:42:39.323 --> 00:42:40.868
He wasn't the only one
who put some money

971
00:42:40.892 --> 00:42:42.884
on the Wellingtons. sir.

972
00:42:42.927 --> 00:42:44.919
And not just $50.

973
00:42:46.631 --> 00:42:48.463
- Mr. Farrell.

974
00:42:48.499 --> 00:42:50.491
You're a gambling
man. it would seem.

975
00:42:52.804 --> 00:42:54.636
- I've made a few bets.

976
00:42:54.672 --> 00:42:57.608
- But you've lost most of them.

977
00:42:57.642 --> 00:43:00.305
In fact. you had to
borrow the $2.000

978
00:43:00.344 --> 00:43:02.904
to place on the Cup challenge.

979
00:43:02.980 --> 00:43:05.176
- I'd have made good on it.
- How?

980
00:43:05.249 --> 00:43:06.493
You've squandered
all of your money.

981
00:43:06.517 --> 00:43:08.577
You have no profession.

982
00:43:10.021 --> 00:43:11.785
Of course. with ten-to-one odds.

983
00:43:11.823 --> 00:43:13.416
you stood to make
a small fortune

984
00:43:13.458 --> 00:43:15.859
if the Wellingtons won.

985
00:43:15.927 --> 00:43:18.692
But that would have required
that Eddie Driscoll play.

986
00:43:18.763 --> 00:43:22.928
And had it been discovered
that Eddie was being paid.

987
00:43:22.967 --> 00:43:24.367
you would have been ruined.

988
00:43:27.105 --> 00:43:28.971
According to your statement.

989
00:43:29.006 --> 00:43:30.975
you stayed after
the team practice

990
00:43:31.008 --> 00:43:34.035
to work on your wrist shot.

991
00:43:36.848 --> 00:43:39.340
That's when you saw Simpson
going into Wallace's office.

992
00:43:39.383 --> 00:43:40.612
isn't it?

993
00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:44.112
And you got curious.

994
00:43:47.125 --> 00:43:48.684
- Wallace is paying Eddie. Sam.

995
00:43:48.726 --> 00:43:50.490
I got the proof right here.

996
00:43:50.528 --> 00:43:52.463
- You panicked.

997
00:43:52.497 --> 00:43:55.729
But then you remembered
Simpson's earlier injury

998
00:43:55.800 --> 00:43:59.396
and realized you could
use it to your advantage.

999
00:43:59.470 --> 00:44:00.961
At first. I didn't
think it was you.

1000
00:44:01.005 --> 00:44:03.372
because you're not tall enough.

1001
00:44:03.407 --> 00:44:07.071
But you would have been. had
you been wearing hockey skates.

1002
00:44:15.820 --> 00:44:19.348
- That's all speculation.

1003
00:44:19.390 --> 00:44:21.052
You have no proof.

1004
00:44:21.092 --> 00:44:24.494
- And you have no alibi.

1005
00:44:24.529 --> 00:44:26.828
Your stick was the
murder weapon.

1006
00:44:26.864 --> 00:44:30.323
Your motive is clear.

1007
00:44:30.368 --> 00:44:34.499
On the face of it. it looks
like murder in the first degree.

1008
00:44:34.539 --> 00:44:38.169
But should you confess
in your own words

1009
00:44:38.209 --> 00:44:43.113
that this was a
rash. unplanned act.

1010
00:44:43.181 --> 00:44:46.015
you may be spared the noose.

1011
00:44:48.786 --> 00:44:51.551
- I was way over
my head in debt.

1012
00:44:53.191 --> 00:44:54.853
and I was counting
on the Wellingtons' win

1013
00:44:54.892 --> 00:44:56.622
to square it off.

1014
00:44:59.297 --> 00:45:01.391
I couldn't afford
for us to lose.

1015
00:45:05.636 --> 00:45:07.070
I had to.

1016
00:45:08.806 --> 00:45:11.105
It would have
disgraced my family.

1017
00:45:13.778 --> 00:45:16.111
But I've done that
now. haven't I?

1018
00:45:19.317 --> 00:45:20.808
- Game on. lads!

1019
00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:24.452
- Yeah. George. yeah.

1020
00:45:24.488 --> 00:45:25.979
- Take us out. Worseley.

1021
00:45:26.057 --> 00:45:27.923
- Ooh. hoo. hoo. hoo.

1022
00:45:27.959 --> 00:45:31.088
- Oh. blood hell.
Worseley. Watch that. lad.

1023
00:45:31.128 --> 00:45:32.152
- Right. then. George.

1024
00:45:32.230 --> 00:45:33.289
Better mind yourself.

1025
00:45:33.331 --> 00:45:34.708
I am going to make
you look the fool.

1026
00:45:34.732 --> 00:45:36.012
- Oh. we'll see about that. sir.

1027
00:45:40.338 --> 00:45:42.000
- Get it. George!

1028
00:45:42.073 --> 00:45:44.440
[cheering]

1029
00:45:44.475 --> 00:45:45.443
- What was that
you were saying. sir?

1030
00:45:45.476 --> 00:45:46.500
- Nice move. George.

1031
00:45:46.577 --> 00:45:47.621
- Who taught you
that. Eddie Driscoll?

1032
00:45:47.645 --> 00:45:49.113
- No. sir. I taught
<i>him</i> that one.

1033
00:45:49.146 --> 00:45:51.672
Although no one could do it
like Constable Deke. eh. boys?

1034
00:45:51.749 --> 00:45:52.749
[laughter]

1035
00:45:59.924 --> 00:46:00.948
- Whoa!

1036
00:46:00.992 --> 00:46:02.620
- Oh!

1037
00:46:02.660 --> 00:46:04.322
- Through my legs. Murdoch.

1038
00:46:04.362 --> 00:46:05.606
- Thank you for
letting that one in. sir.

1039
00:46:05.630 --> 00:46:06.996
- Oh.
- Don't worry. Inspector.

1040
00:46:07.031 --> 00:46:09.432
I'm just letting him
build some confidence.

1041
00:46:09.467 --> 00:46:10.526
[horse neighing]

1042
00:46:10.601 --> 00:46:12.502
- Wagon!
- Wagon.

1043
00:46:22.346 --> 00:46:24.144
So where's Eddie now. George?

1044
00:46:24.181 --> 00:46:27.151
- Sir. he's in Pittsburgh
earning $6 a game.

1045
00:46:27.184 --> 00:46:29.847
- $6? That's madness.

1046
00:46:29.887 --> 00:46:30.786
- He thinks he'll
earn more. sir.

1047
00:46:30.821 --> 00:46:32.687
Maybe up to $10 somewhere else.

1048
00:46:32.723 --> 00:46:34.453
- Bloody hell.
- $10.

1049
00:46:34.492 --> 00:46:36.324
I doubt that. George.

1050
00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:38.158
But still. getting paid to play.

1051
00:46:38.195 --> 00:46:39.857
he's a lucky man.

1052
00:46:39.897 --> 00:46:41.297
- All right. game on!

1053
00:46:41.332 --> 00:46:42.391
- Play.

1054
00:46:45.303 --> 00:46:47.568
[dog whimpering]

1055
00:46:47.638 --> 00:46:48.867
- Oh!

1056
00:46:48.906 --> 00:46:50.101
- Yes!
- Oh!

1057
00:46:50.908 --> 00:46:53.901
